```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>",
                      fig.path = "figures/multivariate-",
                      fig.width = 8, fig.height = 5, dpi = 80,
                      out.width = "100%")
```

This vignette simulates five correlated traits on a random pedigree with a known
genetic covariance matrix `G`, fits the multivariate model with `lmm_animal()`,
and compares the posterior `G`, `E`, `P`, genetic correlations, heritabilities
and fixed effects with the simulated truth.

```{r packages, message = FALSE}
library(stanimal)
library(pedtools)   # randomPed(); install with pak::pkg_install("cran/pedtools")
library(patchwork)
library(ggplot2)
```

## Simulation

`random_covariance(method = "partial")` draws a random covariance matrix (the
package port of `evolqg::RandomMatrix(LKJ = FALSE)`). The residual covariance is
the identity.

```{r simulate}
ped <- randomPed(1500, 50, seed = 2)
ped <- data.frame(id = ped$ID, sire = ped$FIDX, dam = ped$MIDX)
A <- makeA(ped)
L_A <- chol(A)

n_traits <- 5
traits <- paste0("trait", seq_len(n_traits))

set.seed(41)
(G <- random_covariance(n_traits, method = "partial", trait_names = traits))
E <- diag(n_traits)

n <- nrow(A)
a <- t(L_A) %*% matrix(rnorm(n * n_traits), n, n_traits) %*% chol(G)

beta <- matrix(c(rep(1, n_traits),
                 rnorm(n_traits, 0, 1),
                 rnorm(n_traits, 1, 1)), 3, n_traits, byrow = TRUE,
               dimnames = list(c("Intercept", "sex", "Z"), traits))
X <- cbind(Intercept = 1, sex = sample(c(0, 1), n, replace = TRUE),
           Z = rnorm(n))
e <- matrix(rnorm(n * n_traits), n, n_traits) %*% chol(E)

Y <- X %*% beta + a + e
colnames(Y) <- traits
```

## Fit

```{r fit, message = FALSE}
fit <- lmm_animal(Y, X, A, lkj_prior = 2,
                  iter = 3000, warmup = 2000, chains = 4, cores = 4,
                  refresh = 0, seed = 1)
```

The fit is a `CmdStanMCMC` object: summaries and diagnostics use its methods, and
the draws work with `posterior` and `bayesplot`.

```{r summary}
fit$summary(c("h2", "corrG"))
fit$diagnostic_summary()
```

`fit_draws()` returns every quantity as an array with the traits first and the
draws last, with named dimensions.

```{r draws}
draws <- fit_draws(fit)
draws
```

## Posterior means against the truth

`draws_mean()` and `draws_quantile()` give matrices back. Residual correlations
are not a model output; `fit_draws()` derives them from the `E` draws.

```{r means}
round(draws_mean(fit, "G"), 2); round(G, 2)
round(draws_mean(fit, "E"), 2)
round(draws_mean(fit, "corrP"), 2); round(cov2cor(G + E), 2)
round(draws_quantile(fit, "corrE", probs = c(0.05, 0.95)), 2)
```

The fixed effects, with rows as traits:

```{r beta}
round(draws_mean(fit, "beta"), 2)
t(beta)
```

## Intervals against the truth

In these plots the filled circle is the posterior mean, the thick and thin
segments are the 80% and 99% intervals, and the simulated value is the hollow
triangle.

```{r intervals, fig.height = 4}
bayesplot::color_scheme_set("blue")
interval_plot <- function(pars, truth) {
  bayesplot::mcmc_recover_intervals(
    fit$draws(pars), true = stats::setNames(truth, pars),
    prob = 0.8, prob_outer = 0.99, point_est = "mean")
}
pairs <- which(lower.tri(diag(n_traits)), arr.ind = TRUE)
interval_plot(sprintf("h2[%d]", seq_len(n_traits)),
              diag(G) / (diag(G) + diag(E)))
interval_plot(sprintf("corrG[%d,%d]", pairs[, 1], pairs[, 2]),
              cov2cor(G)[pairs])
```

## Prior against posterior

Where the prior and the posterior overlap, the data add little information.

```{r prior-posterior, fig.height = 6}
plot_prior_posterior(fit, trait_names = traits, ncol = 3)
```

## Genetic ellipses and correlations

`plot_G_ellipses()` shows the residuals after the fixed effects with posterior
draws of the `G` ellipses, and the genetic correlations.

```{r ellipses, fig.height = 8}
plot_G_ellipses(fit, Y, n_show = 100, X = X)
```

```{r corr, fig.height = 4}
plot_corr(draws_mean(fit, "corrG"), "Estimated") +
  plot_corr(cov2cor(G), "True")
```

## A derived quantity: eigenvalue variance

Any function of `G` has a posterior: apply it to every draw. Here, the relative
variance of the eigenvalues of `G`, a common measure of integration.

```{r eigenvar, fig.height = 3.5}
eig_var <- apply(draws$G, 3, evolqg::CalcEigenVar)
ggplot(data.frame(value = eig_var), aes(value)) +
  geom_density(fill = "grey70", colour = NA) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = evolqg::CalcEigenVar(G),
             colour = "red", linetype = "dashed") +
  labs(x = "Eigenvalue variance of G", y = "Density",
       title = "Posterior eigenvalue variance of G (dashed = true value)") +
  theme_minimal()
```
